pasta includes or derives from the following projects.

noodles (https://github.com/zaeleus/noodles)
  Copyright (c) 2018 Michael Macias. MIT License (see LICENSE).
  The pasta library began as a copy of the noodles-core, -bgzf, -csi, -fasta, -sam, -bam, -cram,
  -vcf, -bcf, -tabix, and -util crates, with changes made in the DylanCosto/noodles fork.

samtools and HTSlib (https://github.com/samtools)
  Copyright (C) Genome Research Ltd. and contributors. samtools and most of HTSlib are under the
  MIT/Expat License; HTSlib's CRAM code is under the Modified (3-clause) BSD License.
  ntools and parts of pasta (the pileup engine, BAQ, MD/NM calculation, CRAM encoding and
  decoding rules, and the command implementations) are Rust ports of their algorithms and
  behavior, written from their 1.24 sources.

htscodecs (https://github.com/samtools/htscodecs)
  Copyright (C) Genome Research Ltd. Modified (3-clause) BSD License, except the range coder and
  rANS primitives, which are in the public domain (see its licence for the exact files). pasta's
  CRAM codecs (rANS 4x8 and Nx16, the adaptive arithmetic coder, fqzcomp, and the name
  tokenizer) are Rust ports of the htscodecs 1.6 code bundled with HTSlib 1.24.

bcftools (https://github.com/samtools/bcftools)
  Copyright (C) Genome Research Ltd. and contributors. bcftools is under the MIT/Expat License,
  and under the GPL when built with the GNU Scientific Library (GSL), which its `polysomy`
  command needs; vtools has no `polysomy`, and ports nothing that uses the GSL.
  vtools and pasta's variant module (VCF/BCF headers and records, VCF parsing and formatting,
  BCF encoding, indexing, region handling, and the command implementations) are Rust ports of
  the behavior of bcftools and HTSlib, written from their 1.24 sources.

edlib (https://github.com/Martinsos/edlib)
  Copyright (c) 2014 Martin Šošić. MIT License. vtools' `mpileup --indels-cns` edit distances
  are a Rust port of the cut-down C translation of edlib that bcftools 1.24 includes (edlib.c);
  its licence is in bcftools' LICENSE ("License for edlib.[ch]").

VariantKey (https://github.com/tecnickcom/variantkey)
  Copyright (c) 2017-2018 GENOMICS plc, Copyright (c) 2018-2023 Nicola Asuni - Tecnick.com.
  MIT License. vtools' VariantKey conversion is a Rust port of the variantkey.h that bcftools
  1.24 includes.

Release archives also contain licenses/, with the complete samtools, HTSlib, htscodecs,
bcftools (which includes edlib's), and VariantKey licence texts, and THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.txt, with the licence and
notice files of every crate compiled into the binaries (among them libdeflate, which the default
build bundles).
